==Old English==

===Etymology===
{{dercat|ang|gem-pro|inh=1}}
From {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*þurban||to need}}.

Germanic cognates include {{cog|ofs|*thurva}}, {{cog|osx|thurƀan}}, {{cog|odt|thurvan}}, {{cog|goh|durfan}}, {{cog|non|þurfa}}, {{cog|got|𐌸𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌱𐌰𐌽}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|þurfan}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb}}

# to [[need]] {{+obj|ang|gen}}
# to be [[obliged]] (to do something)

====Conjugation====
{{ang-conj|þurfan<pp>}}

====Usage notes====
* ''Þurfan'' is not used in the passive voice, i.e. to express that something "is/was needed", as it lacks an attested past participle. Instead, one would say that there "is/was need of it" using the related noun {{m|ang|þearf|}}, along with the genitive of the thing needed, the dative of the one experiencing the need, and the verb "to be": ''Us wæs þīne þearf'' ("You were needed by us", or more literally, "To us, there was need of you").

